An entity is a distinguishable configuration whose identity and operational definition emerge through dynamic coupling with other entities. The degree and nature of its distinguishability are constrained by the observing entity’s DOS, capabilities, information, purpose, authority, measurement precision, temporal state, environmental conditions, and degree of intentional participation.

No finite coupling necessarily provides a complete definition of an entity. Instead, each coupling produces a contextually sufficient resolution of the entity for the relevant purpose and decision boundary. Different entities, operating under different DOS, capabilities, purposes, resolutions, or states of intentional participation, may therefore assign different but simultaneously valid identities or distinctions to the same underlying configuration.

Entity identity may persist, transform, merge, divide, emerge, or cease across time as configurations and dynamic couplings change. Continuity of causal lineage does not require preservation of the entity’s prior properties or identity at every resolution.

Accordingly, identity is not necessarily an absolute property of an entity, but an evolving relational characterization of an entity under a particular configuration of coupling, observation, and purpose.
